{
  "thread_id": "synth-p3-s6-000",
  "title": "Why are the modified Macdonald stationary weights positive?",
  "question": "In node p3-s6 we require the stationary weights \\pi(\\mu)=F_{\\mu}(x;1,t)/P_{\\lambda}(x;1,t) to be strictly positive for x_i>0 and 0\\le t<1. The proof outline cites Macdonald positivity but does not spell out how it applies to the modified polynomials and the finite sector sum. Could someone explain why both F_{\\mu}(x;1,t) and P_{\\lambda}(x;1,t)=\\sum_{\\nu\\in S_n(\\lambda)}F_{\\nu}(x;1,t) are positive real numbers in that parameter range, ensuring that the stationary distribution is well-defined?",
  "answer": "At q=1 the modified Macdonald polynomial admits the Haglund-Haiman-Loehr lattice-path expansion\\n\\n\\qquad F_{\\mu}(x;1,t)=\\sum_{T\\in\\operatorname{SSYT}(\\mu)} x^{\\mathrm{wt}(T)} t^{\\mathrm{maj}(T)},\\n\\nwhere the sum runs over semistandard Young tableaux of shape \\mu, the monomial x^{\\mathrm{wt}(T)} uses the positive variables x_i, and the major index statistic \\mathrm{maj}(T) is nonnegative. For x_i>0 and 0\\le t<1 every term of the sum is positive, hence F_{\\mu}(x;1,t)>0.\\n\\nThe normalization constant is a finite sum over the sector S_n(\\lambda):\\n\\n\\qquad P_{\\lambda}(x;1,t)=\\sum_{\\nu\\in S_n(\\lambda)}F_{\\nu}(x;1,t).\\n\\nEach summand is strictly positive by the same argument, and there are only |S_n(\\lambda)| summands because the sector is a finite content class. Therefore P_{\\lambda}(x;1,t)>0, so the ratio \\pi(\\mu)=F_{\\mu}(x;1,t)/P_{\\lambda}(x;1,t) defines a legitimate probability weight for every \\mu\\in S_n(\\lambda).",
